{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the man took a golden nose-ring, a bekah in weight, and two bracelets, etc.</b> Rashi explained: a bekah alludes to the shekels of Israel, a bekah per head, and the two bracelets allude to the two tablets, [whose weight was] ten gold [shekels], corresponding to the ten commandments that are upon them. And all these things require sufficient explanation: what connection do the shekels and the tablets have to each other? And perhaps he foresaw by the holy spirit that the tablets were destined to be broken because the evil eye held sway over the first tablets, since they were given publicly and with great fanfare. And perhaps those who gave the evil eye over the tablets will be punished measure for measure, that is, with the evil eye itself. Therefore the shekels were hinted to her — the shekels that protect against the evil eye. And because Eliezer tested Rebecca whether she was possessed of a good eye and found her so, therefore he gave her the bekah, which atones for the evil eye and protects against it. For every possessor of a beautiful eye is saved from the evil eye — proof from Joseph, who was possessed of a beautiful eye, and from there shepherds the stone of Israel. Therefore he was blessed with the blessing, ״בֵּן פֹּרָת עֲלֵי עָיִן״ (Genesis 49:22) — read it ״עוֹלֵי עָיִן״, they rise above the eye, so that the evil eye does not hold sway over any who comes from the seed of Joseph. And likewise Rebecca: in the merit of her being possessed of a good eye, she merited the commandment of the shekels, whose purpose is to save from the evil eye at the time of the census." "And further, she is worthy to receive the ten commandments, as the liturgical poet of Shavuot set forth, and this is his language: ״These commandments are all included in 'you shall not covet,'״ for every possessor of a good eye who gives of his own — it is obvious that he does not covet to take what belongs to others. And you must understand how all the commandments are included in 'you shall not covet': for set the latter commandments from 'you shall not murder' onward — did not Ahab, on account of Naboth the Jezreelite, covet, and also murder, and hire false witnesses, and steal the verdict of the court, and 'you shall not commit adultery' — that is 'you shall not covet your fellow's wife'? But the first commandments, what is their connection to 'you shall not covet'? And it appears to me, that since the commandment of 'honor your father and your mother' seals all five first commandments and encompasses them together — for they all speak of the honor of Heaven — and from the end of a matter one recognizes that its beginning is true, for the honor of Heaven is established by an a fortiori argument from the honor of father and mother. And the tablets were five opposite five, and if so, just as the five latter commandments are included in 'you shall not covet,' so all of them are included in it — for the commandments were all situated opposite one another. And if so, according to this reasoning the statement 'you shall not covet' does not include them all together except through the joining of the two tablets. Therefore it says ״וּשְׁנֵי צְמִידִים עַל יָדֶיהָ״ — that is, joined specifically, for through the joining of the tablets, five opposite five, all the commandments are included in 'you shall not covet.'" "And if you examine precisely the language of צְמִידִים ('bracelets') and the language of בֶּקַע ('a half-shekel'), you will find that they are two opposites: for בֶּקַע is one thing that is split into two, while שְׁנֵי צְמִידִים ('two bracelets') means two separate things that become joined and connected. By this he hinted that her trade is better than the trade of silver and desirous things, for the acquisition of silver is always split into halves — for no man departs from the world with half his craving in his hand: he has a maneh in hand and longs for two hundred. And therefore it is called מָמוֹן ('money'), for the revealed is half of the hidden — when you write מ״ם מ״ם ו״ו נו״ן, it is a hint: if he has forty (מ) zuz in hand, he craves yet another forty (מ), and so on for all of them. But the acquisition of Torah is altogether joined and connected, and multiplies peace to join two that are separate, as it is written (Psalms 119:165) ״שָׁלוֹם רָב לְאֹהֲבֵי תוֹרָתֶךָ״. And at the hour of his passing, nothing accompanies a man but Torah and good deeds, and 'accompany' (מְלַוִּין) is a term of joining and closeness. And if so, wealth is hinted at in בֶּקַע, for a man never has more than half in his hand, whereas the commandment is שְׁנֵי צְמִידִים, for on the contrary — one commandment draws after it yet another whole commandment. And wealth, by its mouth, will be the cause of every quarrel and every affliction, and among brothers it will divide, whereas Torah — all her paths are peace. And wealth is separated and split from a man and does not accompany him, whereas the acquisition of Torah — it will accompany him in all his toil."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar cites Rashi on Genesis 24:22, who derives the allusions from Midrash Tanchuma and Bereshit Rabbah. The phrase רֹעֶה אֶבֶן יִשְׂרָאֵל is itself a prooftext from Genesis 49:24, here applied to Joseph's legacy.", 2 "The wordplay on מָמוֹן resolves the letters as מ-מ-ו-ן = 40+40+6+50, interpreting מ״ם as the letter mem (=40) repeated and נו״ן as the letters vav-nun. The phrase 'the revealed is half of the hidden' plays on reading each letter of ממון as partly visible, partly concealed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:58.885111235Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}